lding and was on his way to a meeting  that was close to the public later on he  did I'm told answer four questions  although I didn't know about that um and  but but he was just entering the  building it's a public space and  reporters do this all the time as he was  walking down the hall I reached out my  phone to him had my phone recording and  I asked him you know this question and  he didn't say anything in that kind of  situation you're never sure whether the  the person you're trying to talk to is  doesn't want to say anything or they're  ignoring you or whether maybe they just  didn't hear you or you didn't hear their  response so I asked him a couple more  times and you know nobody pulled me back  nobody told me you know stay away from  secretary price nobody you know grabbed  my arm  um no one even really identified  themselves as a law enforcement until I  was arrested and I was I was pulled off  taken to a room at the Capitol in  handcuffs and they sent me a way to end  up in an orange jumpsuit ultimately you  were arrested for willful disruption of  state government processes what what  exactly does that mean as far as you've  investigated and and at what point did  it become clear that it was sort of the  the volume and way in which you asked  this question that caused this entire  thing I really have no idea what that  law means I mean my lawyer says that it  may be unconstitutionally vague and I I  think he may be right about that it may  have been in court basically what this  is is the law that they use to if  somebody for example is in the gallery  of the legislature during the  legislative session or interrupts and  meeting  or something and you know shout out and  you know tries to disrupt the meeting  that they can be you know arrested using  this um it's a problematic statute and  the other thing then this is something  else my lawyers pointed out that's kind  of problematic is what process what  governmental process does was i  interrupting hmm  I mean the man was walking down a  hallway I wasn't it was like he was you  know meeting with the governor and some  kind of you know during a thunderstorm  or you know is it it gives it a  grandiosity that doesn't really make  sense and and my my suspicion is is that  the police the Capitol Police who  arrested me I don't think there was any  kind of a an order come down from on  high you know keep the keep the press  away from this guy um there were some  protesters there and the the Trump  administration and secretary price has  been very tightly controlling their  public access regarding this this health  care legislation they don't really like  talking about it and damn time to get a  few with just before we move off of the  the specifics of the airlin number one  or were you sort of noticeably  identifiable as a reporter like did you  have a press pass for example or  something identifying public news  service right I had a press pass on my  chest on the lanyard and I had a shirt  on that said public News Service  now I because it had been raining I also  had a jacket on so I don't think my  shirt was visible but when the police  arrested me I told him I was a reporter  and I told them that I was just doing my  job and they arrested me anyway did you  at any point make any physical contact  with the secretary no now I was never  close enough to make physical contact  with the secretary and in fact later on  he's done some interviews he told  conservative talk show host that the the  talk-show host asked him you know was I  quote Menace  and price said no not really  he wasn't there and I barely noticed him  you know and and this step that kind of  fits because as I said reporters do this  all the time I mean if you google Tom  price you know talking to reporters and  hallways you could pull up you know  images of him answering questions  walking down hallway in Washington DC  and then there's one quote I saw that  says from from some authority I'm not  sure exactly who it is that's quoted  that you were because you were  aggressively breaching personal space  that you were actually removed quote a  couple of times from the area is that  true where you had you already been  removed before this incident no um I  think that was that's actually in the  criminal report and I think that that  may be a case of the Capitol policeman  trying to make this sound as good as  possible  I was not removed from the hallway this  all took place in the in the hallway in  one continuous more or less motion and I  was not removed from that hallway we  were all still in the same room yeah  no one pulled me back no one grabbed me  by my arm  no one pulled me away no one said don't  do that anymore on the I think the only  thing that we can assume they mean there  is because I was trying to reach past  who I assumed at the time was secretary  prices staff although in retrospect they  may have been some of them may have been  Secret Service agents yeah they were not  identified as such being well Secret  Service agents um but I was trying to  reach past them with my phone and they  were you know pushing against me and and  I was you know it did that a few times  and then I tried to sort of jockey  around the entourage to try and get a  better angle on secretary price but no I  was never removed last thing dan what  are the repercussions for you legally  now that you've been charged  with willful disruption of state  government processes well this is a  misdemeanor but it's a it's a pretty  stout one I face  I think the magistrate said a year my  lawyer said that really means six months  but there's a potential of six months in  jail and I think a $500 fine it was  $5,000 bail Wow right yeah my network  put up I think they put up 10% of that  arm but it's it's a you know it's not to  be underestimated  I don't I'm not I think most juries are  going to stand with a free press I mean  in this country we really believe in  reporters being able 